 "desc_header": "IN THIS LESSON",
 "lyrics": "Arctic fox: the white phase has a thick, white coat during the colder, winter months; in summer the coat ranges from gray to brown on the back; paws are sheathed in dense fur during the winter; dense, bushy coat; long, fluffy tail (SeaWorld Parks & Entertainment animal info 'Arctic Fox', seaworld.org/animals/facts/mammals/arctic-fox, read Nov 17 2026).\nPolar bear: hair is see-through with no pigment, so it looks white; black nose, skin and claws; covered with fur except for the nose; subcutaneous fat 5-10 cm thick in adults; ears relatively small; tail 7-12 cm; even foot pads may be furred during winter (SDZWA Library fact sheet 'Polar Bear', Physical Characteristics, ielc.libguides.com/sdzg/factsheets/polarbear/characteristics, read Nov 17 2026). Thick fat layer and fur to stay warm in extreme Arctic temperatures (fact sheet summary page, read Nov 17 2026).\nBrown bear: in cold climates undergo winter dormancy; farthest north habitats up to 7 months; dens in dug earth have a tunnel about 4 feet long leading to a chamber 5 feet in diameter; dens are excavated as early as September or immediately before use in November; often stand while stopped; relies mainly on a sense of smell (SDZWA Library fact sheet 'Brown Bear', Behavior & Ecology, ielc.libguides.com/sdzg/factsheets/brownbear/behavior, read Nov 17 2026).",
 "script": "Hello, friends! I am Milo the bluebird.\nToday we are learning about how animals get ready for winter!\nCoats, fat and cozy dens. Who does what?\nThree animal friends will show us. Count them with me. One, two, three!\nPut on your warm coat. Let's go!\nWho grows a thick, white coat for the snowy winter?\nIs it the arctic fox? Or is it the pig?\nIt is the arctic fox!\nHere is the fun fact. In winter, this arctic fox has a thick, white coat.\nWhite, like the snow all around it. Fluffy and warm! Can you spot it in the snow?\nWhat color is its coat in summer, when the snow is gone? Hmm.\nIn summer, its coat turns gray and brown on its back!\nLook at that fluffy, white fox!\nIn winter, its paws are covered in thick fur too. Furry paws, like warm boots!\nFurry paws, on the cold, cold snow. Tip, tap, tip, tap.\nAnd it has a long, fluffy tail. So bushy, and so soft!\nWhite coat, furry paws, and a fluffy tail. This fox is ready for winter!\nSay it with me. An arctic fox has a white winter coat!\nCan you tiptoe like a fox on the snow? Tip, tap, tip, tap!\nNow wag a big, fluffy tail. Swish, swish!\nQuick check! What color is the arctic fox's winter coat? White? Or purple?\nWhite! Like the snow. Great job!\nWho has fur that looks white, with black skin underneath?\nIs it the polar bear? Or is it the cow?\nIt is the polar bear!\nHere is the fun fact. Under its fur, a polar bear's skin is black!\nIts fur looks white, but each hair is see-through, like glass. Wow! Isn't that amazing?\nWhat else keeps a polar bear warm, under its skin? Hmm.\nA thick layer of fat! Up to four inches thick. Warm and cozy!\nLook at that big, fluffy bear!\nThick fur and thick fat keep it warm, in the freezing cold Arctic.\nFur everywhere, except its nose. Its nose is black, too! Black nose, black skin!\nA polar bear has small ears, and a short little tail.\nSmall ears, a short tail, and a big, fluffy body! What a bear!\nSay it with me. A polar bear has black skin!\nCan you hug yourself, warm and cozy like a polar bear? Squeeze!\nNow touch your nose. Boop! A polar bear's nose is black.\nQuick check! Is a polar bear's skin black? Yes, or no?\nYes! Black skin, under white-looking fur. You remembered!\nWho sleeps in a cozy den, all winter long?\nIs it the brown bear? Or is it the pig?\nIt is the brown bear!\nHere is the fun fact. In cold places, a brown bear spends the winter in a den.\nA den is a cozy room, under the ground. Snug and safe!\nHow long can a brown bear stay in its den? Hmm.\nIn the coldest places, up to seven months! That is most of the year.\nLook at that sleepy brown bear!\nA brown bear digs its own den. A tunnel, then a round room at the end.\nSome bears start digging their den in September! Dig, dig, dig!\nA brown bear uses its nose to learn about the world around it. Sniff, sniff!\nAnd a brown bear often stands up tall, on its back legs, to look around.\nSay it with me. A brown bear sleeps in a den!\nCan you dig like a bear? Dig, dig, dig, with both paws!\nNow curl up in your den, and take a little nap. Shh.\nQuick check! Where does a brown bear spend the winter? In a den? Or up a tree?\nIn a den! Cozy and snug. Super thinking!\nWow! We met three friends who are ready for winter. Now it is quiz time!\nGet ready. Think hard!\nWho grows a white coat for the snow?\nThe arctic fox! Yes!\nWho has black skin under its fur?\nThe polar bear! That's right!\nWho sleeps in a den all winter?\nThe brown bear! You remembered!\nYou learned so many new things today.\nA white coat like a fox. Thick fat like a polar bear. And a cozy den like a brown bear!\nWhat do you wear when it is cold outside?\nThank you for learning with me. Bye-bye, friends! See you next time!",
